179 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 85 0 R
/E (The AI-Powered Insect Detection and Pesticide Recommendation System is a smart agriculture solution designed to assist farmers in identifying crop pests quickly and accurately using artificial intelligence and computer vision technologies. The project follows a practical and user-friendly approach, focusing on real-time pest detection, sustainable farming practices, and easy accessibility for farmers through mobile and web platforms. The main objective of the system is to reduce crop damage, minimize excessive pesticide usage, and improve agricultural productivity through intelligent decision-making.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
180 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 85 0 R
/E (Unlike traditional pest identification methods that depend heavily on manual inspection and agricultural experts, the proposed system provides an automated and cost-effective solution capable of detecting insects directly from uploaded crop images. The system uses deep learning techniques, especially Convolutional Neural Networks \(CNN\), to classify insects based on their visual features and provide suitable pesticide recommendations according to the detected pest type and infestation level.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
181 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 85 0 R
/E (The application allows farmers to capture or upload crop images through a simple interface, after which the AI model processes the image and generates real-time results. The system displays the detected insect name, confidence level, recommended pesticides, preventive measures, and usage instructions. This enables farmers to take faster and more informed actions for crop protection.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
182 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 85 0 R
/E (Built using technologies such as Python, TensorFlow/PyTorch, OpenCV, and cloud-based databases, the project emphasizes accuracy, lightweight processing, and efficient performance. The implementation also focuses on scalability, ease of use, and environmental sustainability by promoting targeted pesticide application instead of excessive chemical use.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
